<p>I like that the ending of this manages to be both charming and wistful. I'm a little disappointed that we didn't get to see 5090-C in action :&lt;</p>
<p>I ought to point out some of the linguistic errors which I felt detracted from the description. Hope you don't mind!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Open</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>A faraday cage</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Faraday&quot; should be capitalised</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No evidence of external hardware which operates SCP-5090 exists, but is instead entirely operated by SCP-5090-1.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This makes it sound like the verb &quot;operated&quot; refers to the nonexistent external hardware. A possible rewording would be: &quot;No evidence of external hardware which operates SCP-5090 exists; instead, SCP-5090 is entirely operated by SCP-5090-1.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One or more of these websites will appear in a <em>users</em> browser history <em>and or</em> bookmarks</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>user's</em> (with apostrophe), <em>and/or</em> (with slash)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-5090-A is a <em>blog style</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>blog-style</em> (hyphenated)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Despite <em>it's</em> intended purpose, SCP-5090-B contains</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>its</em> (no apostrophe)</p>
</div>
